{
  "duration": 457.27200000001284,
  "chapters": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 96.45600000000276
    },
    {
      "start": 96.45600000000276,
      "end": 192.5040000000055
    },
    {
      "start": 192.5040000000055,
      "end": 277.46400000000784
    },
    {
      "start": 277.46400000000784,
      "end": 366.3120000000103
    },
    {
      "start": 366.3120000000103,
      "end": 457.27200000001284
    }
  ],
  "cues": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 1.4600000381469727,
      "text": "Hey, welcome back.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 2.0799999237060547,
      "end": 5.179999828338623,
      "text": "Our last class showed how a magnetic field turns a current loop.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 6.039999961853027,
      "end": 9.5,
      "text": "Today, we use that turning effect to measure a small steady current.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 9.760000228881836,
      "end": 14.84000015258789,
      "text": "A moving coil galvanometer contains a light coil with many turns, a pointer,",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 15.359999656677246,
      "end": 18.459999084472656,
      "text": "a magnetic system and a restoring suspension or spring.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 19.139999389648438,
      "end": 20.540000915527344,
      "text": "Current produces a magnetic torque.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 21.239999771118164,
      "end": 25.098975157126404,
      "text": "The spring resists rotation, and the pointer stops where those torques",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 25.098975157126404,
      "end": 25.540000915527344,
      "text": "balance.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 26.100000381469727,
      "end": 28.5,
      "text": "The magnetic system is designed carefully.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 29.399999618530273,
      "end": 33.5,
      "text": "Curved pole pieces and a soft iron core produce an approximately radial field",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 33.5,
      "end": 34.459999084472656,
      "text": "in the working region.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 35.119998931884766,
      "end": 39.68000030517578,
      "text": "As the coil turns, the local field stays in its plane, perpendicular to the",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 39.68000030517578,
      "end": 40.15999984741211,
      "text": "coil's normal.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 40.84000015258789,
      "end": 45.20000076293945,
      "text": "The sine factor in the magnetic torque is therefore one throughout the useful",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 45.20000076293945,
      "end": 46.02000045776367,
      "text": "deflection range.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 46.779998779296875,
      "end": 50.71230844350961,
      "text": "This field is not uniform in direction across the instrument; we use the",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 50.71230844350961,
      "end": 51.040000915527344,
      "text": "radial",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 51.040000915527344,
      "end": 55.459999084472656,
      "text": "field model with approximately constant magnitude at the active coil sides.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 56.459999084472656,
      "end": 59.060001373291016,
      "text": "Magnetic torque is N I A B.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 59.81999969482422,
      "end": 64.0999984741211,
      "text": "If c is restoring torque per radian, spring torque magnitude is c times theta.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 64.83999633789062,
      "end": 68.95999908447266,
      "text": "At equilibrium, c theta equals N I A B.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 69.66000366210938,
      "end": 74.31999969482422,
      "text": "So theta equals N A B over c, multiplied by current.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 75.19999694824219,
      "end": 81.16000366210938,
      "text": "Double the current within the operating range and the",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 81.16000366210938,
      "end": 81.69999694824219,
      "text": "deflection doubles. Notice the two different angles.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 81.69999694824219,
      "end": 84.66000366210938,
      "text": "The pointer's deflection changes, while the normal to local field angle stays",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 84.66000366210938,
      "end": 85.5199966430664,
      "text": "ninety degrees.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 86.33999633789062,
      "end": 90.18000030517578,
      "text": "Confusing them would incorrectly insert sine of pointer deflection into the",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 90.18000030517578,
      "end": 90.91999816894531,
      "text": "instrument equation.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 91.86000061035156,
      "end": 95.63999938964844,
      "text": "Damping helps the pointer settle; our equation describes its final steady",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 95.63999938964844,
      "end": 96.45600000000276,
      "text": "reading, not every instant during motion.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 96.45600000000276,
      "end": 98.1960000095395,
      "text": "How responsive is the pointer?",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 99.15600004768648,
      "end": 103.47599998092927,
      "text": "We define current sensitivity here as deflection divided by current.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 104.45600000000276,
      "end": 109.79600015259065,
      "text": "It is N A B over c, measured in radians per ampere if we use angular units.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 111.05600038147249,
      "end": 115.63600030517854,
      "text": "Larger turn count, larger area or stronger field increases it when the other",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 115.63600030517854,
      "end": 116.43599954223909,
      "text": "quantities are fixed.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 117.15600076294221,
      "end": 119.83599916076936,
      "text": "A softer restoring spring also increases it.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 120.5959993896512,
      "end": 125.07600083923616,
      "text": "Take one hundred turns, area two square centimeters, field zero point two",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 125.07600083923616,
      "end": 130.1721521178053,
      "text": "tesla, and restoring constant four times ten to the minus six newton meters",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 130.1721521178053,
      "end": 130.37599816894806,
      "text": "per",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 130.37599816894806,
      "end": 130.59599938965118,
      "text": "radian.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 131.71599832153595,
      "end": 135.47600045776642,
      "text": "Two square centimeters is two times ten to the minus four square meters.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 135.47600045776642,
      "end": 140.7559992370633,
      "text": "N A B is zero point zero zero four, giving current sensitivity one thousand",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 140.7559992370633,
      "end": 141.69600167846954,
      "text": "radians per ampere.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 142.39599862671173,
      "end": 146.70112897393918,
      "text": "A half milliampere produces zero point five radians of deflection in this",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 146.70112897393918,
      "end": 146.9960009155301,
      "text": "ideal",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 146.9960009155301,
      "end": 147.59599938965118,
      "text": "linear model.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 148.13600030517853,
      "end": 151.21599832153595,
      "text": "Now let the bare coil resistance G be one hundred ohms.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 151.61599984741486,
      "end": 153.51600137329376,
      "text": "Its voltage is current times G.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 154.63600030517853,
      "end": 158.63600030517853,
      "text": "Voltage sensitivity is therefore current sensitivity divided by G, or ten",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 158.63600030517853,
      "end": 159.37599816894806,
      "text": "radians per volt.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 159.9159990844754,
      "end": 163.67600122070587,
      "text": "That half milliampere needs zero point zero five volts across the coil.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 164.95600000000275,
      "end": 166.63600030517853,
      "text": "Here is a useful exam trap.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 167.35600152588165,
      "end": 170.55599847412384,
      "text": "Double the number of turns while holding area, field and restoring constant",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 170.55599847412384,
      "end": 170.97599664306915,
      "text": "fixed.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 172.11600366211212,
      "end": 173.19599786377228,
      "text": "Current sensitivity doubles.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 173.896002441409,
      "end": 178.07600274658478,
      "text": "If the longer winding also doubles resistance, voltage sensitivity stays",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 178.07600274658478,
      "end": 181.23599877929962,
      "text": "unchanged, because numerator and resistance both double.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 182.09599938965118,
      "end": 185.09599938965118,
      "text": "Greater current sensitivity alone does not guarantee greater voltage",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 185.09599938965118,
      "end": 185.57600274658478,
      "text": "sensitivity.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 186.4960009155301,
      "end": 190.11138758498245,
      "text": "Keep coil resistance separate from the restoring constant, and keep radians",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 190.11138758498245,
      "end": 190.25600305176056,
      "text": "per",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 190.25600305176056,
      "end": 192.15599694824493,
      "text": "ampere separate from radians per volt.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 192.5040000000055,
      "end": 196.04399996185853,
      "text": "A delicate galvanometer cannot carry a large current directly.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 196.94400005722596,
      "end": 201.36399965668275,
      "text": "To make an ammeter, put a small shunt resistance S in parallel with its coil.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 202.14400034332826,
      "end": 205.8440001525934,
      "text": "Most of the current uses the shunt, while a controlled small fraction passes",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 205.8440001525934,
      "end": 206.70399980927064,
      "text": "through the galvanometer.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 207.92400007629945,
      "end": 211.5639994659479,
      "text": "Let full scale coil current be I g and coil resistance be G.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 212.0639994659479,
      "end": 216.79938467603404,
      "text": "If the desired full scale total current is I, the shunt current is I minus I",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 216.79938467603404,
      "end": 216.92400007629945,
      "text": "g.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 216.92400007629945,
      "end": 218.784000686651,
      "text": "Parallel branches share the same voltage.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 219.32399969482972,
      "end": 223.284000686651,
      "text": "So I g times G equals I minus I g, multiplied by S.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 223.82399969482972,
      "end": 228.62399893189027,
      "text": "Solve for S: I g G divided by I minus I g.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 228.62399893189027,
      "end": 232.04400091553285,
      "text": "Let's use a different instrument with full scale current one milliampere and",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 232.04400091553285,
      "end": 233.48399954224183,
      "text": "coil resistance one hundred ohms.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 234.1039984741266,
      "end": 235.48399954224183,
      "text": "We want a one ampere range.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 236.0840018310602,
      "end": 240.28399877930238,
      "text": "At full scale the coil voltage is zero point one volt, and the shunt carries",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 240.28399877930238,
      "end": 242.1039984741266,
      "text": "zero point nine nine nine amperes.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 242.42399816895082,
      "end": 246.4439986267145,
      "text": "The required shunt is approximately zero point one zero zero one ohms.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 247.72400122070863,
      "end": 251.62399893189027,
      "text": "The complete ammeter's resistance is voltage divided by total current, giving",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 251.62399893189027,
      "end": 252.80399923706605,
      "text": "zero point one ohms.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 253.92399816895082,
      "end": 257.98400335693907,
      "text": "Do not round I minus I g to I until you know the approximation is acceptable.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 258.70399694824766,
      "end": 260.3040030517633,
      "text": "A second check is the circuit connection.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 261.0040000000055,
      "end": 264.6240027465875,
      "text": "The shunt is parallel to the coil inside the instrument, but the complete",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 264.6240027465875,
      "end": 267.58400183106016,
      "text": "ammeter is connected in series with the branch whose current we measure.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 268.3439963378961,
      "end": 270.6439993896539,
      "text": "Those two statements refer to different parts of the circuit.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 270.6439993896539,
      "end": 275.6439993896539,
      "text": "We assume steady direct current, constant resistances and negligible lead",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 275.6439993896539,
      "end": 276.96399908447813,
      "text": "resistance for these calculations.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 277.46400000000784,
      "end": 279.78399993325064,
      "text": "Now suppose we want to measure voltage.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 280.7439999713976,
      "end": 284.50399996186087,
      "text": "Connect a large resistance R in series with the coil to limit the current.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 285.12399984741995,
      "end": 288.94399954224417,
      "text": "The complete voltmeter is then connected across the two points whose potential",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 288.94399954224417,
      "end": 289.7640001907427,
      "text": "difference we want.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 290.52400041962454,
      "end": 294.8339998855669,
      "text": "Same instrument: full scale coil current one milliampere, resistance one",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 294.8339998855669,
      "end": 295.3639996185381,
      "text": "hundred ohms.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 295.9840004577715,
      "end": 300.3240006103594,
      "text": "For a ten volt range, total resistance must be voltage divided by full scale",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 300.3240006103594,
      "end": 302.1440003051836,
      "text": "current, or ten thousand ohms.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 302.8240006103594,
      "end": 307.24400068665335,
      "text": "The coil already contributes one hundred ohms, so the added series resistance",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 307.24400068665335,
      "end": 309.28399969483206,
      "text": "is nine thousand nine hundred ohms.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 310.1039993896563,
      "end": 314.20323115853336,
      "text": "At full scale, the coil drops zero point one volt and the series resistor",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 314.20323115853336,
      "end": 314.4840004577715,
      "text": "drops",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 314.4840004577715,
      "end": 315.6039993896563,
      "text": "nine point nine volts.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 316.30400015259573,
      "end": 317.80400015259573,
      "text": "Together they make ten volts.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 318.80400015259573,
      "end": 321.5839989318926,
      "text": "Writing ten thousand ohms as the added resistance would count the coil",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 321.5839989318926,
      "end": 322.46400000000784,
      "text": "resistance twice.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 323.0839989318926,
      "end": 326.7439987793047,
      "text": "In symbols, V equals I g times G plus R.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 327.6039993896563,
      "end": 330.70400167847464,
      "text": "Rearranging gives R equal to V over I g, minus G.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 331.06399847412894,
      "end": 335.1039993896563,
      "text": "This design requires a target range at least as large as the bare coil's full",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 335.1039993896563,
      "end": 338.9239990844805,
      "text": "scale voltage if we use only a nonnegative added series resistance.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 339.6440003051836,
      "end": 343.2340004577715,
      "text": "For a twenty volt range with the same coil, total resistance becomes twenty",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 343.2340004577715,
      "end": 346.88399816895316,
      "text": "thousand ohms and the added resistance becomes nineteen thousand nine hundred",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 346.88399816895316,
      "end": 347.26400305176566,
      "text": "ohms.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 348.1039993896563,
      "end": 352.6240036621172,
      "text": "Remember the two levels of connection: multiplier and coil are internally in",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 352.6240036621172,
      "end": 356.66399694825003,
      "text": "series, but the complete voltmeter is externally in parallel with the measured",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 356.66399694825003,
      "end": 357.1039993896563,
      "text": "component.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 357.7439987793047,
      "end": 361.98399664307425,
      "text": "A voltmeter should draw little current, so high input resistance is desirable.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 361.98399664307425,
      "end": 366.04400183106253,
      "text": "It is still a real branch of the circuit, not an invisible observer.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 366.3120000000103,
      "end": 369.2920000190838,
      "text": "Let's see why meter resistance matters.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 370.03200002862053,
      "end": 372.3519999618633,
      "text": "An ideal ten volt source feeds a ten ohm resistor.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 373.1720001335247,
      "end": 375.6520001525982,
      "text": "Without a meter, current is one ampere.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 376.1920001144512,
      "end": 378.5919997329815,
      "text": "Insert our zero point one ohm ammeter in series.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 379.372000419627,
      "end": 383.69700022889214,
      "text": "Total resistance becomes ten point one ohms, so current is about zero point",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 383.69700022889214,
      "end": 385.11199923707085,
      "text": "nine nine zero amperes.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 385.8120000000103,
      "end": 389.0920006866558,
      "text": "The instrument correctly reads the current in the altered circuit, which is",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 389.0920006866558,
      "end": 390.8120000000103,
      "text": "slightly smaller than the original current.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 391.3719994659527,
      "end": 392.54199954224663,
      "text": "Now a voltage example.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 392.8420006866558,
      "end": 397.04199954224663,
      "text": "An ideal ten volt source feeds two ten thousand ohm resistors in series.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 397.7219998474224,
      "end": 401.0620000000103,
      "text": "The voltage across the second resistor is five volts before measurement.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 402.082000457774,
      "end": 404.642001831065,
      "text": "Connect our ten thousand ohm voltmeter across that second resistor.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 405.44200106812553,
      "end": 409.02199908448296,
      "text": "Two equal ten thousand ohm branches in parallel give five thousand ohms.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 409.8020016784771,
      "end": 413.8419987793072,
      "text": "The loaded divider now has ten thousand ohms above and five thousand ohms",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 413.8419987793072,
      "end": 414.1619984741314,
      "text": "below.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 414.642001831065,
      "end": 419.2799489135845,
      "text": "Its measured voltage is ten times five thousand divided by fifteen thousand,",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 419.2799489135845,
      "end": 419.4020001525982,
      "text": "or",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 419.4020001525982,
      "end": 421.02199908448296,
      "text": "about three point three three volts.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 421.9620015258892,
      "end": 425.60200091553764,
      "text": "The drop from five volts is a loading effect, not a mistake in Ohm's law.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 426.4020001525982,
      "end": 429.3819996948345,
      "text": "A much higher voltmeter resistance would disturb this circuit less.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 430.1619984741314,
      "end": 433.94199725342827,
      "text": "This is why an ideal ammeter has zero resistance and an ideal voltmeter has",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 433.94199725342827,
      "end": 434.7420003051861,
      "text": "infinite resistance.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 435.5819966430767,
      "end": 439.09558694224285,
      "text": "Also, do not connect a low resistance ammeter directly across a voltage",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 439.09558694224285,
      "end": 439.44199725342827,
      "text": "source;",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 439.8419987793072,
      "end": 441.20199938965874,
      "text": "it can draw excessive current.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 441.94199725342827,
      "end": 446.846104958569,
      "text": "For JEE problems, redraw the actual connections, preserve the coil's full",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 446.846104958569,
      "end": 447.18200274659233,
      "text": "scale",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 447.18200274659233,
      "end": 450.8620030517681,
      "text": "current, and use branch voltages and currents to derive the formulas.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 451.68200274659233,
      "end": 455.3419987793072,
      "text": "Our next class will turn from measuring instruments to magnetic dipole fields",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 455.3419987793072,
      "end": 456.80199786377983,
      "text": "and bar magnet geometry.",
      "chapter": 4
    }
  ]
}
